# ── 28: OT-07.3, the anger ledger's held items read outward ──
b = Fill(28, "M02")
b.f("H23a", "statement", "Saul's anger is kindled \"when he heard these words\", the threat to Jabesh, with the Spirit of God rushing upon him (1Sa 11:6), and it ends in mercy that gives the day to God: \"Not a man shall be put to death this day, for today the Lord has worked salvation in Israel\" (1Sa 11:13).", [23], verses=["1Sam.11.6", "1Sam.11.13"])
b.f("H23b", "statement", "Samson: the Spirit's rushing goes with the striking at Ashkelon, and the \"hot anger\" comes at the end, with its ground given: his wife wept \"You only hate me; you do not love me\" (Judg 14:16), he told her \"because she pressed him hard\" (14:17), and his own word is \"If you had not plowed with my heifer, you would not have found out my riddle\" (14:18); then \"Samson's wife was given to his companion\" (14:20).", [23], verses=["Judg.14.16", "Judg.14.17", "Judg.14.18", "Judg.14.19", "Judg.14.20"])
b.f("H23c", "statement", "The passage puts Samson's anger (Judg 14:19) with being betrayed through his wife, not with the Spirit; so it differs from Saul's, where the Spirit and the anger come together (1Sa 11:6).", [23], basis="read", verses=["Judg.14.19", "1Sam.11.6"])
b.f("H24", "term", "\"They were enraged\" (Act 7:54) is diapriō (\"to be sawn through\", G1282) with kardia (\"heart\"); \"they ground their teeth at him\" puts the anger in the body; against them stands Stephen, \"full of the Holy Spirit, gazed into heaven\" (7:55); the M47 token is the heart word itself.", [24], verses=["Acts.7.54", "Acts.7.55"])
b.f("H25", "statement", "Two grounds for subjection stand side by side: \"to avoid God's wrath\" and \"for the sake of conscience\" (Rom 13:5).", [25])
b.f("H26", "statement", "\"That their hearts may melt, and many stumble. At all their gates I have given the glittering sword\" (Eze 21:15): the melting heart is what the sword is given for, fear aimed at the heart by God.", [26], basis="read", verses=["Ezek.21.15"])
b.f("H28a", "statement", "Num 20:13 names Meribah: \"where the people of Israel quarreled with the Lord, and through them he showed himself holy\".", [28], verses=["Num.20.13"])
b.f("H28b", "statement", "Rev 18:3 reads \"all nations have drunk the wine of the passion of her sexual immorality\", beside Rev 14:8.", [28], verses=["Rev.18.3", "Rev.14.8"])
b.f("K39", "statement", "The king of Moab \"saw that the battle was going against him\" and tried to break through, \"but they could not\" (2Ki 3:26); \"Then he took his oldest son who was to reign in his place and offered him for a burnt offering on the wall\" (3:27), giving up his own heir and succession at the point of defeat.", [39], verses=["2Kgs.3.26", "2Kgs.3.27"])
b.f("K43", "statement", "The wrath in 2Ki 3:27 is qeṣep (H7110A). Where \"wrath came upon\" a people with no bearer named, the passage gives a breach first (Num 1:53; 18:5; Jos 9:20; 22:20; 1Ch 27:24; 2Ch 24:18; 32:25); in 2Ch 32:25-26 the same wrath is then called \"the wrath of the Lord\"; in 2Ki 3 no breach is named, and Elisha had said \"He will also give the Moabites into your hand\" (3:18).", [43, 44, 45], verses=["Num.1.53", "Num.18.5", "Josh.9.20", "2Chr.32.25", "2Chr.32.26", "2Kgs.3.18", "2Kgs.3.27"])
b.f("K45", "statement", "The outcome in 2Ki 3: \"And they withdrew from him and returned to their own land\"; the campaign ends short of the word.", [45], verses=["2Kgs.3.27"], flag="the outcome paragraph, lines 46-47")
b.f("K45r", "statement", "The verse leaves open whose wrath it is: the construction elsewhere is God's after a breach; the setting would also allow Moab's fury, roused by the sacrifice, or Israel's own horror at it; the verse does not decide.", [45], basis="read", verses=["2Kgs.3.27"], flag="the reading paragraph, lines 48-50")
b.f("G61", "statement", "The teeth are where anger and hostility show, as the hands are for fear: \"The wicked man sees it and is angry; he gnashes his teeth and melts away\" (Psa 112:10); \"The wicked plots against the righteous and gnashes his teeth at him\" (Psa 37:12); and Act 7:54.", [61, 62, 63], flag="the set-based pull of the gnashing words, lines 59-60", verses=["Ps.112.10", "Ps.37.12", "Acts.7.54"])
b.f("G65", "statement", "Gnashing in gloating and mockery: \"All your enemies rail against you; they hiss, they gnash their teeth\" (Lam 2:16); \"like profane mockers at a feast, they gnash at me with their teeth\" (Psa 35:16).", [65], verses=["Lam.2.16", "Ps.35.16"])
b.f("G68", "statement", "God's anger as the sufferer feels it: \"He has torn me in his wrath and hated me; he has gnashed his teeth at me\" (Job 16:9).", [68], verses=["Job.16.9"])
b.f("G69", "statement", "\"Weeping and gnashing of teeth\" stands seven times in the judgment sayings (Mat 8:12; 13:42; 13:50; 22:13; 24:51; 25:30; Luk 13:28); Luke gives the moment: \"when you see Abraham and Isaac and Jacob and all the prophets in the kingdom of God but you yourselves cast out\" (13:28).", [69, 70, 71], verses=["Matt.8.12", "Matt.13.42", "Matt.13.50", "Matt.22.13", "Matt.24.51", "Matt.25.30", "Luke.13.28"])
